---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import LangSwitch from "../components/LangSwitch.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="The same hand pressing the same button five times over, each impression printed further out of register than the last, in flat gold and black on cream — a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <LangSwitch href="/zh/" label="中文" />

  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="thesis">
    Something failed. Should you try again, and how? The answer is usually
    yes, and the retry usually works, which is how the reflex gets learned
    before anyone examines it. Twelve weeks on the cases where it does not
    work: the payment charged twice, the recovering dependency flooded by
    its own clients, the retry still running ninety seconds after the caller
    stopped waiting for the answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Twelve weeks, twelve situations</h2>
  <p>
    Every week takes the same question into a new situation, and each
    situation is concrete enough to reproduce: a read that times out once, a
    payment that is charged twice, ten thousand clients that all come back on
    the same second, two people who resend the same payout at 3am with no
    code between them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every week has the same five parts: the situation, the reflex everyone
    reaches for, why it fails here, what to do instead, and who owns that
    decision — the client, the library, the platform, the operator, the
    product team, or nobody. Every fix has a limit built into how it works.
    Backoff adds latency to requests that were about to succeed. An open
    circuit breaker fails calls that would have worked. A deadline discards
    work that was nearly finished. By week 12 you should be able to state
    the limit on any retry policy you propose and defend it out loud.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Third-year students who have written code that talks to something over a
    network and have already been surprised by it. You need to be comfortable
    reading an unfamiliar codebase and making a small change to it; you do not
    need distributed systems theory, and the course does not assume you have
    run anything in production.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will spend most of the semester making failures happen on purpose,
    then deciding what to do about them. You will leave with an audit, a retry
    policy you have defended out loud against someone briefed to break it, and
    a written argument that some failures should not be retried at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve situations, in order, with the failure each one is built around.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessments" href="/assessments/">
      <p>The audit, the policy and the case, with what each is worth and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course, what to bring them, and when they are free.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .thesis {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin-block: 0 2.5rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1rem;
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--at-accent);
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
